//! **THE FLAT BUILD PROFILE of the riscv32 `virt` board** — SOS's tier-3
//! demonstration (sawos design 35, M5 unit 4; design 19's "the flat tier is a
//! PROFILE, not an architecture"; design 25's tier table, last row).
//!
//! Same architecture, same board, same silicon, same toolchain. What differs is
//! ONE thing: this profile programs the hardware to deny nothing, and it SAYS SO
//! through the tier word. Everything else — the trap frame, the UART, the CLINT
//! timer, the PLIC, the memory map, the linker scripts, the user-side ABI — is
//! reached from the two modules that already own it, so there is no second copy
//! of anything to keep in step.
//!
//! ## The three modules, and why this file is short
//!
//! Design 23 split riscv32 into an ARCH half (`rv32core`, shared by every
//! riscv32 board) and a BOARD half (each board's own `lib.saw`, published as
//! `hal`). This profile is a third level of the same idea — a PROFILE half —
//! and it is built by naming the other two rather than by copying either:
//!
//! ```
//! rv32core   hal/riscv32-common/kernel   the arch: frame, PMP, cause decode
//! rv32virt   hal/riscv32/kernel          the board: UART, CLINT, PLIC, map
//! hal        hal/riscv32-flat/kernel     THIS FILE: the protection profile
//! ```
//!
//! **`rv32virt` IS THE VIRT BOARD'S OWN `lib.saw`, UNDER A SECOND MODULE NAME.**
//! Not a copy and not a fork — the identical directory, mapped by the runner as
//! `rv32virt=hal/riscv32/kernel` for this profile where it maps it as
//! `hal=hal/riscv32/kernel` for the isolated one. A Saw module's name comes from
//! its `--module-path` mapping, so one directory can be the board's `hal` in one
//! build and a component of somebody else's `hal` in another. That is the whole
//! trick, and it is what keeps design 23's consolidation intact: `riscv32-common`
//! is still the one arch home, `hal/riscv32/` is still the one virt board, and
//! adding this profile edited neither.
//!
//! The arch names are taken from `rv32core` DIRECTLY rather than through
//! `rv32virt`, though `rv32virt` re-exports them too. Two reasons, and the
//! second is the load-bearing one: a two-level re-export is the shape design 23
//! probed and proved, and — more to the point — the names this profile must NOT
//! inherit are exactly the protection ones, so taking the arch half by name
//! makes the omission visible in one list instead of hidden in a chain.
//!
//! ## What "flat" means here, precisely
//!
//! `prot_isolated()` answers `false` and `SystemOp.TierGet` answers `Flat`. Per design
//! 19, carried whole:
//!
//! - `map` SUCCEEDS HONESTLY. Its contract is "make this region accessible with
//!   at least these rights"; on this profile every region already is, so the
//!   contract holds and the call is not a lie. The kernel still records the row,
//!   still charges the quota, still hands back a Mapping that still owns it.
//! - `unmap`'s REVOCATION HALF IS WHAT IS DISCLAIMED. The row leaves the record
//!   and the Mapping dies; the hardware goes on permitting the access.
//! - PEER ISOLATION IS DISCLAIMED. A process can reach another's memory.
//! - THE KERNEL BOUNDARY IS NOT DISCLAIMED. Handles are checked, rights are
//!   enforced, quotas count and lifetimes end, exactly as on the isolated
//!   profile. That is why the object-model suite runs here unchanged and passes.
//!
//! ## PMP is OPENED, not left alone — the one thing worth knowing
//!
//! "Leave PMP open" cannot mean "write nothing". RISC-V gives default-DENY for
//! free: if no PMP entry matches a U-mode access AND at least one entry is
//! implemented, the access fails. At reset every `pmpcfg` byte is zero (A=OFF),
//! so a profile that simply emptied the `prot_*` bodies would deny root its own
//! first instruction. Flat therefore takes ONE affirmative action — a single TOR
//! entry spanning the whole address space, RWX — and that is the only hardware
//! this file touches.
//!
//! It is published from `prot_commit` AND `prot_switch`, both of which are
//! stateless and idempotent, because those are the two seam calls that survive
//! `prot_replay_at_switch()` being false on the paths that matter: `load_domain` calls
//! `prot_switch` unconditionally, and the pre-M2 harness kernels under `tests/`
//! program the seam directly and end at `prot_commit`. Covering both means no
//! new HAL hook and no `kernel/core` edit — and three CSR writes on a path that
//! runs a handful of times per process is not a cost worth a boot flag.
//!
//! The act itself is `rv32core.prot_open_all`, not a body here: PMP is
//! ARCHITECTURAL, so the register writes live in the arch home beside
//! `prot_reset`, whose exact opposite they are. This file decides WHEN the
//! hardware is opened; `rv32core` knows HOW.

// THE ARCH HALF, RE-EXPORTED — minus the protection surface, which is this
// profile's whole reason to exist. What is deliberately ABSENT from this list,
// and defined below instead: `prot_reset`, `prot_region`, `prot_device`,
// `prot_commit`, `prot_switch`, `prot_update`, `prot_clear`,
// `prot_replay_at_switch` and `prot_isolated` — the last two of which are the
// BOARD's rather than the arch's, so they are absent from this list because
// `rv32core` no longer has them to give.
//
// `PROT_GRAIN`, `GRANT_ROW_BUDGET`, `PROT_DOMAIN_SLOTS` and `device_window_ok`
// DO come across unchanged, and that is deliberate rather than incidental: a
// flat platform has no hardware wall, but the kernel's static asserts price a
// grant record against these numbers and a profile that moved them would be
// testing a different kernel. `device_window_ok` in particular still REFUSES a
// malformed window here — the flat tier disclaims denial by the HARDWARE, not
// the kernel's own argument checking, and `iomemory_carve` proves it.
public import rv32core.{
    arch_tag, cause_tag, device_window_ok, FRAME_BYTES, frame_init,
    GRANT_ROW_BUDGET, image_link_base, IRQ_NONE, IRQ_TIMER, is_interrupt,
    is_syscall, map_place,
    MAX_ROOT_SEGMENTS, payload_end, payload_start, phys_to_virt,
    PROT_DOMAIN_SLOTS, PROT_GRAIN,
    region_table_end, region_table_start,
    resume_frame, syscall_arg0, syscall_arg1, syscall_arg2, syscall_handle,
    syscall_op, syscall_return, trap_pc, virt_to_phys,
}

// THE BOARD HALF, RE-EXPORTED FROM THE VIRT BOARD ITSELF. Every one of these is
// a fact about the QEMU `virt` machine — where its UART is, how its CLINT
// counts, which PLIC context it wires, where its RAM begins and where root is
// linked — and none of them changes because the protection profile did. The
// board is the same board.
//
// `arch_name` is the ONE public name of the board's that is NOT taken: this
// profile announces itself, below, because a boot banner that could not tell
// the two profiles apart would make a transcript diff read the same for two
// different machines.
public import rv32virt.{
    console_byte, DEVICE_GRANT_BASE, DEVICE_GRANT_LEN, exit_fail, exit_pass,
    intc_init, irq_claim, irq_complete, irq_line_valid, irq_mask, irq_poll,
    irq_raise_selftest_line, irq_unmask, map_source_ok, map_target_ok,
    ROOT_LOAD_BASE, ROOT_REGION_TOP, ROOT_STACK_LEN, timer_now_ns,
    timer_pending, timer_set_deadline_ns, UNMAPPED_PROBE, wait_for_irq,
}

// THE ONE ROUTINE THIS PROFILE CALLS THAT IT DOES NOT RE-EXPORT. `prot_open_all`
// is `rv32core`'s — PMP is architectural, and that body would be identical on
// any RV32 M/U part with PMP — and it is reached through a PLAIN import because
// `kernel/core` has no business with it: the seam `kcore` sees is the seven
// `prot_*` functions below, and opening the hardware is how THIS profile
// implements two of them.
//
// It is `rv32core`'s for a second reason worth knowing before anyone moves it
// here: the CSR writes are `extern "C"` leaves, an extern carries no visibility
// and cannot be re-exported (SL-6), and two modules that both DECLARE one C
// symbol collide across the whole compilation unit. One owner, one declaration.
import rv32core.{prot_open_all}

// =============================================================================
// Identity
// =============================================================================

/// What the boot banner says. It NAMES THE PROFILE, which the isolated board's
/// answer ("riscv32 (QEMU virt)") deliberately does not need to.
///
/// The harness matches its banner expectation as a PREFIX, so the arch word at
/// the front is what the assertion reads and the parenthesis is for the human
/// reading a transcript. A flat kernel and an isolated one boot on the same
/// machine and differ in exactly one advertised property; a reader who cannot
/// see which one produced a log cannot check anything about it.
public func arch_name() -> String {
    "riscv32 (QEMU virt, flat profile)"
}

// =============================================================================
// The protection seam — the whole of this profile
// =============================================================================

/// **DOES THIS PROFILE REBUILD ITS PROTECTION IMAGE AT EVERY SWITCH?** No —
/// there is nothing to rebuild. (A function and not a `static`: see SL-25 and
/// the board's note — a `static` is a name the whole program shares, so a
/// profile could not override one.)
///
/// `false` retires `load_domain`'s replay loop entirely: the kernel keeps its
/// grant record (teardown and compaction still walk it, and the quota ledger
/// still prices it), and simply never programs hardware from it. The constant
/// folds, so the loop is not emitted here at all.
public func prot_replay_at_switch() -> Bool { false }

/// **CAN THIS PLATFORM DENY?** No. This is the tier word (sawos design 35), and
/// it is the one sentence this whole directory exists to be able to say
/// honestly.
///
/// `kernel/core` maps it to `sosabi.ProtectionTier.Flat` and `SystemOp.TierGet`
/// hands it to userspace, where a program that relies on isolation reads it once
/// and refuses to run. Design 19's rule, restated at the declaration: the
/// difference between the tiers is ADVERTISED, never faked. Every `prot_*` body
/// below is empty or permissive; this word is what makes that honest rather than
/// broken.
public func prot_isolated() -> Bool { false }

/// Nothing to reset — this profile installs one permission and never varies it.
/// The isolated profile clears sixteen entries here because it is about to
/// rebuild them from the record; there is no record-driven image to rebuild.
public func prot_reset() unsafe {
}

/// A no-op. The grant row is in the kernel's record, which stays authoritative
/// at every tier; this profile just never turns a row into a hardware region.
public func prot_region(idx: UInt, base: UInt, top: UInt, perms: UInt8) unsafe {
}

/// A no-op, for `prot_region`'s reason. A device window is as reachable as
/// everything else here.
public func prot_device(idx: UInt, base: UInt, len: UInt) unsafe {
}

/// **PUBLISH THE PERMISSIVE CONFIGURATION.** One of the two places
/// `rv32core.prot_open_all` is reached from.
///
/// This is the call the pre-M2 harness kernels under `tests/` end their
/// protection setup with — they program `prot_reset`/`prot_region`/`prot_commit`
/// directly rather than through `load_domain`, so `prot_switch` alone would
/// leave them with a dead PMP and a U-mode payload that faults on its first
/// instruction. Publishing here costs three CSR writes and keeps every one of
/// those cases running on this profile.
public func prot_commit() unsafe {
    prot_open_all()
}

/// **PUBLISH THE PERMISSIVE CONFIGURATION.** The other place, and the one the
/// real kernel takes.
///
/// `load_domain` calls this unconditionally — it is outside the
/// `prot_replay_at_switch()` guard — so it is the one seam call guaranteed to run
/// before root ever enters U-mode, and it runs again at every process switch
/// after that. Doing the work every time rather than once behind a flag keeps
/// this profile STATELESS: there is no "have we opened it yet" word to get wrong
/// on a path nobody tested.
public func prot_switch(p: UInt) unsafe {
    prot_open_all()
}

/// A no-op — with the isolated profile's one assertion KEPT.
///
/// The flat tier does not translate either, so design 33's invariant holds here
/// exactly as it does on PMP: a user address IS its physical address, and a row
/// whose `pa` had drifted from its `base` would mean the placement seams
/// disagreed. Keeping the check costs one comparison per grant and catches a
/// class of bug that a profile programming no hardware would otherwise be unable
/// to notice at all — which is the argument for keeping it here rather than
/// against it.
public func prot_update(p: UInt, base: UInt, top: UInt, pa: UInt, perms: UInt8,
                        device: Bool) unsafe {
    assert(pa == base,
           "a user address on the flat tier IS its physical address")
}

/// Nothing to retire. `clear_domain` has already emptied the kernel's record,
/// which is the only place this profile's protection state ever lived.
public func prot_clear(p: UInt) unsafe {
}
